  "title": "Have you eaten?",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "The **present perfect** ('have done') adds **-এছ-** + the person ending:\n\n- করা → **করেছি** 'I have done'\n- খাওয়া → **খেয়েছি** 'I have eaten', **খেয়েছো** 'you have eaten', **খেয়েছেন** 'you have eaten (polite)'\n- যাওয়া → **গিয়েছে** 'has gone'\n- আসা → **এসেছে** 'has come' (you met **এসেছি** in *আমি আমেরিকা থেকে এসেছি*)\n\nBengali uses the perfect much more than English, for anything just finished or still relevant now. **মাত্র** (*matro*) 'just' fits naturally: **আমি মাত্র খেয়েছি** 'I've just eaten'.\n\nDon't confuse three look-alikes: **করছি** 'I am doing', **করেছি** 'I have done', **করলাম** 'I did'.\n\nIn everyday speech the perfect of যাওয়া is often shortened: **গিয়েছে → গেছে** (*gechhe*). You'll hear both.\n\n**Culture:** **খেয়েছেন?** / **ভাত খেয়েছো?** ('Have you eaten (rice)?') is a warm everyday greeting in Bangladesh, especially from elders. It shows care more than curiosity. Answer **জি, খেয়েছি** and ask back.\n\n**কী হয়েছে?** 'What happened? / What's wrong?' is one of the most useful perfect-tense chunks.",
